# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Full Slides Data for Chapter 15: Data Through Pictures"""

slides = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Raw Data Collection', 'tagline': 'Systematic Field Inquiry • Gathering TV Viewing Hours Across Thirty-Five Friends', 'lecture': 'Welcome to our mathematical investigation of data collection, systematic organization, and visual interpretation. When curious young researchers set out to understand everyday human habits, they begin with a direct inquiry. In this school survey, two student reporters interviewed thirty-five classmates to measure daily television viewing duration. As each classmate answered, the raw individual responses were written down directly into a handheld field notebook. Some friends reported watching television for only half an hour each afternoon after completing homework and outdoor chores. Other children recorded one full hour, while several engaged for one and a half hours or two hours. A few classmates admitted spending more than two hours glued to the television screen every single evening. At first glance, a notebook filled with scattered numbers, varied scribbles, and handwritten notes looks overwhelming and disordered. Without proper organization, it is impossible to quickly answer fundamental questions, such as which viewing duration is most common. Raw data represents unorganized factual numbers collected directly from the original source before any mathematical sorting occurs. To transform these scattered observations into meaningful mathematical knowledge, we must structure the findings into clear categories. Careful data collection provides the empirical bedrock upon which all statistical reasoning, logical deductions, and sound conclusions are built.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">What is Raw Data?</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Raw data consists of initial, unprocessed numbers and facts collected directly through field surveys, observations, or measurements before grouping or statistical analysis.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">The 35-Friend TV Viewing Survey</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Two student investigators surveyed 35 classmates regarding their daily television screen time. Raw notebook responses included:</p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-200">\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">½ hr, 1 hr, 1½ hrs, 2 hrs, >2 hrs</div>\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">Total Surveyed = 35 Children</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Raw data requires systematic categorization into time intervals before meaningful patterns can emerge.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the primary defining characteristic of raw data in mathematical statistics?', 'options': ['It is already organized into a finished visual graph', 'It consists of initial unorganized numbers collected directly from observation', 'It must always equal exactly one hundred data points', 'It only contains numbers that have been multiplied together'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Raw data represents initial unorganized facts and figures collected directly from source observations before any grouping or analysis.', 'hint': 'Think about what a notebook looks like before you create tables or charts.'}, {'question': 'If 35 friends are surveyed in total, what must the sum of all response frequencies equal?', 'options': ['Exactly 35', 'Fewer than 30', 'Always 50', 'Exactly 100'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Every surveyed classmate contributes exactly one response, so the total sum across all frequency categories must equal the survey sample size of 35.', 'hint': 'No responses are lost or duplicated when tallying the full group.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Frequency Distribution Table', 'tagline': 'Organizing Observations • Tally Marks and Mutually Exclusive Time Intervals', 'lecture': 'Now let us examine how to structure raw notebook responses into an organized frequency distribution table. A frequency table groups scattered observations into clear, mutually exclusive intervals so that every single data point belongs somewhere. For our television survey, we establish five distinct categories of daily screen time across the cohort. The first category is half an hour, followed by one hour, one and a half hours, two hours, and finally more than two hours. To count accurately without skipping or double-counting, we record each response using standardized tally marks. Four vertical lines are drawn one by one, and every fifth entry is recorded as a diagonal slash across the four lines. This bundle of five allows our eyes to quickly count large totals in convenient groups of five. When all thirty-five children are tallied, we write the final numerical frequency beside each time category. For instance, five children watch half an hour, twelve children watch one hour, eight watch one and a half hours, six watch two hours, and four watch more than two hours. Adding five, twelve, eight, six, and four yields exactly thirty-five, verifying our complete survey tally. Frequency distribution tables transform raw chaotic lists into clear, readable, and highly informative mathematical structures.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Standard Tally Marks Grouping</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Tally marks count occurrences in bundles of 5: four vertical strokes (||||) crossed by a diagonal line (<s>||||</s> = 5). This makes large totals rapid to count.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Frequency Table of Daily TV Viewing</span>\n                <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-1">\n                    <table class="w-full text-left font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300 border-collapse">\n                        <thead>\n                            <tr class="border-b border-slate-700 text-amber-300">\n                                <th class="py-1">Duration</th>\n                                <th class="py-1">Tally Marks</th>\n                                <th class="py-1 text-right">Frequency</th>\n                            </tr>\n                        </thead>\n                        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800">\n                            <tr><td>½ hour</td><td><s>||||</s></td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">5</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>1 hour</td><td><s>||||</s> <s>||||</s> ||</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">12</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>1½ hours</td><td><s>||||</s> |||</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">8</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>2 hours</td><td><s>||||</s> |</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">6</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>> 2 hours</td><td>||||</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">4</td></tr>\n                            <tr class="border-t border-slate-600 font-bold text-emerald-400"><td>Total</td><td>35 Tallies</td><td class="text-right">35</td></tr>\n                        </tbody>\n                    </table>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Check Sum:</strong> 5 + 12 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 35 total classmates surveyed.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many items are represented by two full tally bundles followed by three single marks (<s>||||</s> <s>||||</s> |||)?', 'options': ['10', '13', '15', '8'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Each bundle of five equals 5. Two bundles equal 10, plus 3 individual strokes equals 13 items.', 'hint': 'Count by 5s: 5, 10, then add the three extra marks.'}, {'question': 'Which time duration had the highest frequency of viewers in our 35-classmate survey?', 'options': ['½ hour (5 children)', '1 hour (12 children)', '2 hours (6 children)', 'More than 2 hours (4 children)'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '1 hour had 12 viewers, which is the highest frequency in the distribution table.', 'hint': 'Look for the category with the largest numerical tally.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'TV Survey Analysis', 'tagline': 'Interpreting Statistical Tables • Evaluating Healthy Screen Habits', 'lecture': 'Once our frequency table is assembled, we can extract profound insights regarding daily lifestyle patterns and screen habits. Data analysis involves comparing categories, calculating combined subgroups, and evaluating health recommendations against actual recorded behavior. Looking closely at our television viewing table, we immediately observe that one hour is the most frequent choice, chosen by twelve children. Next, let us determine how many classmates spend one hour or less watching television each day. We combine the five children watching half an hour with the twelve children watching one hour, giving seventeen children in total. Conversely, how many children watch television for more than one hour daily? We sum eight children at one and a half hours, six at two hours, and four at more than two hours, giving eighteen children. Notice that seventeen plus eighteen equals our complete sample of thirty-five friends without any missing entries. Health guidelines generally recommend limiting recreational screen time to no more than one hour daily to safeguard eyesight and physical activity. Our data reveals that eighteen out of thirty-five children exceed this recommended threshold. Statistical tables empower students to transition from vague guesses to factual evidence, illuminating real-world habits with mathematical precision.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Subgroup Cumulative Calculations</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 text-slate-300 font-mono text-[11px]">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">1 Hour or Less:</span> 5 (½ hr) + 12 (1 hr) = 17 children\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">More than 1 Hour:</span> 8 (1½ hrs) + 6 (2 hrs) + 4 (>2 hrs) = 18 children\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="text-slate-400 text-[10px] mt-1 text-center">Sum: 17 + 18 = 35 children (100% of surveyed cohort)</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Evaluating Health Recommendations</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Health experts recommend no more than 1 hour of daily leisure screen time. In our cohort, 18 out of 35 children (over 51%) exceed this limit, highlighting an important lifestyle consideration.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/30 text-sky-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Analytical Power:</strong> Tables allow combining multiple intervals to evaluate broader societal questions.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many children watch television for one hour or less per day in the survey?', 'options': ['12 children', '17 children', '18 children', '5 children'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '5 children watch ½ hour and 12 watch 1 hour. Combined: 5 + 12 = 17 children watch one hour or less.', 'hint': 'Add the counts for the ½ hour and 1 hour categories together.'}, {'question': 'What fraction of the 35 surveyed children watch television for more than two hours daily?', 'options': ['4 out of 35', '12 out of 35', '18 out of 35', '6 out of 35'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'The table records exactly 4 children in the category of more than two hours, which is 4/35 of the cohort.', 'hint': 'Check the last row of the frequency table.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Stock Pictograph Scaling', 'tagline': 'Visual Data Encoding • One Icon Represents Five Physical Play Items', 'lecture': 'Now let us explore pictographs, where numerical quantities are represented visually using scaled symbols and meaningful icons. Consider a neighbourhood toy shop preparing its inventory stock before summer vacation begins for schoolchildren. The shopkeeper counts hundreds of play items, including stuffed teddy bears, traditional board games, and various sports equipment. Drawing dozens of individual pictures on an inventory sheet would take far too long and clutter the paper. Instead, cartographers and mathematicians define a key or scale factor to represent multiple physical objects compactly. In this toy shop inventory pictograph, the shopkeeper decides that one single icon represents five physical items. Therefore, if we see four toy icons in the toys row, we calculate four multiplied by five, which equals twenty toys in stock. If the board games row displays six game icons, the total quantity equals six multiplied by five, giving thirty board games. If the sports equipment row displays eight ball icons, we multiply eight by five, yielding forty sports items. By establishing a clear scale, a small number of icons compactly represents substantial inventory quantities. Always inspect the key at the bottom or corner of a pictograph before counting, because the scale determines the true values.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">The Essential Role of the Scale / Key</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">A pictograph represents data through pictures. The <b>Key</b> tells the viewer the numerical value represented by each icon symbol.</p>\n                <div class="mt-2 p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 font-mono text-[11px] text-center">\n                    🔑 Scale: 1 Icon (⭐) = 5 Physical Items\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Toy Shop Inventory Calculations</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🧸 Toys: 4 icons × 5</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">20 Toys</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🎲 Board Games: 6 icons × 5</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">30 Games</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>⚽ Sports Gear: 8 icons × 5</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">40 Sports Items</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Total Stock:</strong> 20 + 30 + 40 = 90 total play items in the shop inventory.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 icon represents 5 items, how many total items are represented by 7 icons?', 'options': ['12 items', '25 items', '35 items', '70 items'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Multiply the number of icons by the scale value: 7 × 5 = 35 items.', 'hint': 'Calculate 7 groups of 5.'}, {'question': 'How many icons must be drawn to represent 40 sports equipment items when 1 icon = 5 items?', 'options': ['8 icons', '5 icons', '10 icons', '4 icons'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Divide total items by the scale factor: 40 ÷ 5 = 8 icons.', 'hint': 'Divide 40 by 5.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Custom Scale Pictograph', 'tagline': 'Modifying Representation Scale • Converting Between Scale Factors', 'lecture': 'An essential mathematical skill is choosing and adapting the scale factor of a pictograph to suit our analytical needs. In the previous scenario, our shopkeeper used a scale where one icon represented five physical play items. Under that scale, ninety total items required eighteen separate icons across the three inventory rows on the page. What happens if our paper is smaller, and we wish to use fewer icons for faster drawing and clearer presentation? We can choose an alternative scale factor, such as letting one icon represent ten physical items. Let us recalculate our inventory quantities using this new scale of ten items per icon. For toys, twenty items divided by ten means we need to draw only two icons. For board games, thirty items divided by ten requires exactly three icons. For sports equipment, forty items divided by ten yields four icons. Under a scale of ten, our entire shop inventory is shown using just two plus three plus four, which equals nine icons. Notice that changing the scale does not alter the underlying real-world quantities in the slightest. Twenty toys, thirty games, and forty sports gear items remain identical whether we draw eighteen icons or nine icons. Choosing an appropriate scale balances visual clarity with drafting efficiency.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Comparing Scales for the Same Inventory</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">The underlying data remains constant. Increasing the scale factor reduces the number of icons needed to display the same quantities.</p>\n                <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-2">\n                    <table class="w-full text-left font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300 border-collapse">\n                        <thead>\n                            <tr class="border-b border-slate-700 text-amber-300">\n                                <th class="py-1">Category</th>\n                                <th class="py-1">Count</th>\n                                <th class="py-1 text-center">Scale: 1 = 5</th>\n                                <th class="py-1 text-right">Scale: 1 = 10</th>\n                            </tr>\n                        </thead>\n                        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800">\n                            <tr><td>Toys</td><td>20</td><td class="text-center font-bold text-sky-400">4 icons</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-emerald-400">2 icons</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Board Games</td><td>30</td><td class="text-center font-bold text-sky-400">6 icons</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-emerald-400">3 icons</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Sports Gear</td><td>40</td><td class="text-center font-bold text-sky-400">8 icons</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-emerald-400">4 icons</td></tr>\n                            <tr class="border-t border-slate-600 font-bold"><td>Total Icons</td><td>90</td><td class="text-center text-sky-300">18 icons</td><td class="text-right text-emerald-300">9 icons</td></tr>\n                        </tbody>\n                    </table>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/30 text-sky-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Scaling Principle:</strong> When scale doubles (from 5 to 10), the number of required icons is cut in half (from 18 to 9).\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If a shop has 50 balls and the scale is 1 icon = 10 balls, how many icons must be drawn?', 'options': ['5 icons', '10 icons', '50 icons', '2 icons'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': 'Divide the total count by the scale factor: 50 ÷ 10 = 5 icons.', 'hint': 'Divide 50 by 10.'}, {'question': 'What happens to the total number of drawn icons if the scale factor of each icon is doubled?', 'options': ['The number of icons doubles', 'The number of icons is halved', 'The number of icons stays exactly the same', 'The number of icons increases by ten'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Because each icon now represents twice as many physical items, only half as many icons are needed to represent the same total.', 'hint': 'If one coin buys double the candy, you need half as many coins.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Traffic Tally Pictograph', 'tagline': 'Roadside Vehicle Observation • One Scooter Symbol Represents Three Two-Wheelers', 'lecture': 'Let us apply pictographs to real-world transportation tracking by observing street traffic passing outside a home during rush hour. A student monitored the flow of two-wheelers, such as scooters and motorcycles, during one morning hour on three different days. Observations were systematically conducted on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday along the neighborhood main road. To record the traffic cleanly, the student created a pictograph where one scooter symbol represents three actual two-wheelers. On Monday, the pictograph displays five scooter symbols. Multiplying five by three reveals that fifteen two-wheelers passed along the road on Monday morning. On Wednesday, the road was quieter, and only three scooter symbols were recorded in the observation tally. Three symbols multiplied by three means nine two-wheelers passed on Wednesday. On Friday, traffic surged as commuters hurried for weekend errands, resulting in eight scooter symbols. Eight symbols multiplied by three yields twenty-four two-wheelers on Friday. By scanning the visual symbols, we instantly spot that Friday experienced the heaviest road traffic of all three days. The total number of two-wheelers observed across all three days equals fifteen plus nine plus twenty-four, which totals forty-eight vehicles. Scaled pictographs allow rapid comparison across different observation periods while preserving accurate mathematical data.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Roadside Traffic Observation Data</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">A student observed two-wheelers on a street during morning commute hours on three weekdays.</p>\n                <div class="mt-2 p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 font-mono text-[11px] text-center">\n                    🛵 Key: 1 Scooter Symbol = 3 Two-Wheelers\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Three-Day Traffic Count</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Monday: 5 symbols × 3</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">15 Two-Wheelers</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Wednesday: 3 symbols × 3</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">9 Two-Wheelers</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Friday: 8 symbols × 3</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">24 Two-Wheelers</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Total Observed:</strong> 15 + 9 + 24 = 48 two-wheelers over three morning observation sessions.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many actual two-wheelers passed on Friday if 8 scooter symbols are displayed and 1 symbol = 3 vehicles?', 'options': ['11 vehicles', '24 vehicles', '16 vehicles', '27 vehicles'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Multiply the number of symbols by the scale value: 8 × 3 = 24 vehicles.', 'hint': 'Multiply 8 by 3.'}, {'question': 'How many more two-wheelers passed on Friday (24) than on Wednesday (9)?', 'options': ['15 vehicles', '12 vehicles', '18 vehicles', '9 vehicles'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': "Subtract Wednesday's count from Friday's count: 24 - 9 = 15 vehicles.", 'hint': 'Calculate 24 minus 9.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Road Traffic Comparison', 'tagline': 'Comparative Traffic Analysis • Re-scaling and Fraction Icons', 'lecture': "Let us deepen our comparative analysis of the traffic pictograph and explore re-scaling with fraction symbols. We previously determined that fifteen vehicles passed on Monday, nine on Wednesday, and twenty-four on Friday. Subtracting nine from twenty-four shows that Friday had fifteen more vehicles than Wednesday, which was the quietest day. What if we choose to redraw this pictograph using a larger scale factor, where one icon represents six two-wheelers? For Friday's twenty-four vehicles, dividing twenty-four by six gives four complete icons. For Monday's fifteen vehicles, fifteen divided by six equals two with a remainder of three vehicles. Since three is exactly half of six, we represent Monday with two full icons plus one half-icon. For Wednesday's nine vehicles, nine divided by six equals one with a remainder of three vehicles. Thus Wednesday requires one full icon plus one half-icon to display its traffic accurately. Half-icons are a standard cartographic convention in pictographs whenever quantities fall halfway between whole multiples. Using a scale of six requires only four icons for Friday, two and a half for Monday, and one and a half for Wednesday. Understanding fractional symbols enables accurate visual representation of numbers that do not divide evenly by the chosen scale.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Re-scaling with a Larger Unit (Scale = 6)</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">When data counts do not divide evenly by the scale factor, fractional icons represent the remainder.</p>\n                <div class="mt-2 p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 font-mono text-[11px] text-center">\n                    🛵 Full Icon = 6 Vehicles | 🛺 Half Icon = 3 Vehicles\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Re-scaled Pictograph Representation</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Monday (15): 15 ÷ 6</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">2½ Icons (6 + 6 + 3)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Wednesday (9): 9 ÷ 6</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">1½ Icons (6 + 3)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Friday (24): 24 ÷ 6</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">4 Full Icons</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/30 text-sky-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Fractional Principle:</strong> Half of an icon representing 6 equals exactly 3 units.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 icon represents 6 vehicles, what quantity does a half-icon represent?', 'options': ['1 vehicle', '2 vehicles', '3 vehicles', '6 vehicles'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Half of the scale value is 6 ÷ 2 = 3 vehicles.', 'hint': 'Divide the full scale value by 2.'}, {'question': 'How many icons represent 18 vehicles when 1 icon = 6 vehicles?', 'options': ['2 icons', '3 icons', '4 icons', '6 icons'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Divide 18 by 6: 18 ÷ 6 = 3 full icons.', 'hint': 'Calculate 18 divided by 6.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Icon Daily Schedule', 'tagline': 'Tracking a 24-Hour Day • The One-Icon-Per-Hour Method', 'lecture': 'Every child has exactly twenty-four hours in a day, but how we allocate those hours across activities varies greatly. To understand time management, students can record their daily routines across standard activities: sleeping, school, studying, playing, eating, and chores. One student recorded their daily routine using a direct pictorial list, where each icon represents exactly one hour. For sleeping, the student drew eight bed icons, representing eight hours of restful sleep at night. For school, six book icons were drawn, accounting for six hours spent in the classroom and morning assembly. For personal study and homework at home, two pencil icons were recorded, totaling two hours of focused learning. For eating meals and outdoor play, four ball and plate icons were drawn, representing four hours of nourishment and recreation. Finally, four clock icons accounted for other routine activities like getting dressed, commuting, and family conversations. Summing these hours yields eight plus six plus two plus four plus four, which equals exactly twenty-four hours. This icon method makes it easy to visualize individual hours as tangible blocks across the entire day. However, counting long rows of identical icons can become tedious when comparing multiple categories quickly.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">The 24-Hour Time Budget Principle</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Every single day contains exactly 24 hours. All activities must sum to exactly 24 hours with no unaccounted gaps.</p>\n                <div class="mt-2 p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 font-mono text-[11px] text-center">\n                    ⏰ Total Daily Budget: 24 Hours\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">The Icon Schedule Method (1 Icon = 1 Hour)</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🛏️ Sleeping: 8 icons</span>\n                        <span class="text-indigo-400 font-bold">8 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🏫 School: 6 icons</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">6 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>📖 Self-Study: 2 icons</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">2 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>⚽ Play & Meals: 4 icons</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">4 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>⏳ Other Routines: 4 icons</span>\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">4 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Check Sum:</strong> 8 + 6 + 2 + 4 + 4 = 24 hours complete.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What fraction of the 24-hour day does 8 hours of sleep represent?', 'options': ['8/24 (or 1/3 of the day)', '8/12 (or 2/3 of the day)', '8/20', '1/4 of the day'], 'answer': 0, 'explanation': '8 hours divided by the 24 hours in a full day equals 8/24, which simplifies to 1/3 of the day.', 'hint': 'Divide 8 by 24 and reduce the fraction.'}, {'question': 'If a student spends 6 hours at school and 2 hours on self-study, how many total hours are dedicated to education?', 'options': ['4 hours', '8 hours', '10 hours', '12 hours'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Add school time and study time: 6 + 2 = 8 total educational hours.', 'hint': 'Add 6 and 2.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Routine Bar Graph', 'tagline': 'Visualizing Time With Bars • Standard Cartesian Axes and Proportional Heights', 'lecture': 'To overcome the limitations of counting long rows of identical icons, we transition to vertical bar graphs. A bar graph represents data using rectangular bars of uniform width where the height of each bar is proportional to the quantity. Let us examine how a student constructs a vertical bar graph to display the twenty-four hour daily routine. First, two perpendicular reference lines are drawn: a horizontal base axis and a vertical value axis. Along the horizontal base axis, we label the distinct activity categories: sleeping, school, study, play and meals, and other routines. Along the vertical axis, we establish an evenly spaced numerical scale marked in hours from zero up to ten. Next, rectangular columns of equal width are erected above each category label across the page. The sleeping bar rises vertically until it aligns with eight on the vertical scale. The school bar reaches upward to six hours, while the self-study bar reaches two hours. The play and meals bar and the other routines bar each rise to four hours. With a single glance, our eyes instantly compare relative durations without counting individual symbols. Bar graphs provide immediate visual comparison, making relative differences in magnitude evident across categories.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Anatomy of a Vertical Bar Graph</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">A bar graph represents numerical values using rectangular bars. Key architectural elements include:</p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-200">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block">Horizontal Axis (X):</span> Discrete Categories (Activities)\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block">Vertical Axis (Y):</span> Numerical Scale (Hours 0–10)\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Daily Routine Bar Heights</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🛏️ Sleeping</span><span class="text-indigo-400 font-bold">8 Hours (Tallest Bar)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>🏫 School</span><span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">6 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>📖 Self-Study</span><span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">2 Hours (Shortest Bar)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>⚽ Play & Meals</span><span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">4 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>⏳ Other Routines</span><span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">4 Hours</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Visual Efficiency:</strong> Heights reveal the tallest bar (sleep) and shortest bar (study) instantaneously.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What determines the height of each bar in a vertical bar graph?', 'options': ['The width of the graph paper', 'The numerical value or frequency of the category', 'The number of letters in the category name', 'The color chosen to paint the column'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'In a vertical bar graph, the height of each rectangular bar corresponds directly to the numerical quantity it represents.', 'hint': 'Taller bars represent larger numerical values.'}, {'question': 'Which activity corresponds to the tallest bar in the 24-hour routine graph?', 'options': ['School (6 hours)', 'Self-Study (2 hours)', 'Sleeping (8 hours)', 'Play & Meals (4 hours)'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Sleeping has 8 hours, which is the greatest height among all activities on the vertical axis.', 'hint': 'Identify the activity with the highest numerical value.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Schedule Balance Analysis', 'tagline': 'Evaluating Routine Equity • Sleep-Study Proportions and Personal Planning', 'lecture': "Now let us analyze the daily schedule bar graph to evaluate the balance, equity, and healthfulness of a student's daily routine. Statistical graphs do not merely present numbers; they facilitate critical thinking and informed personal decision-making for young learners. Looking closely at the heights of the bars, we compare the hours dedicated to restorative sleep against active waking pursuits. Sleeping occupies eight hours, which represents exactly one-third of the twenty-four hour day. Medical researchers emphasize that growing children need at least eight to nine hours of sound sleep for healthy brain development and vitality. Next, consider academic learning, which combines six hours of school instruction with two hours of self-study at home. This totals eight hours of intellectual engagement, perfectly matching the eight hours allocated to restful sleep. The remaining eight hours are divided equally between active recreation, nourishment, hygiene, and essential personal care. This division exhibits a remarkably balanced three-part routine: one-third for sleep, one-third for education, and one-third for recreation and daily living. Students are encouraged to chart their own personal twenty-four hour day on grid paper. Plotting personal routines enables young learners to identify imbalances, reduce wasted time, and cultivate healthy lifelong habits.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">The Golden Rule of Thirds (8-8-8 Balance)</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">A healthy 24-hour daily routine often balances three fundamental pillars:</p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 mt-2 text-center font-mono text-[11px]">\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-indigo-950/60 rounded border border-indigo-500/30 text-indigo-300">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-sm">8 Hrs</div>\n                        <div class="text-[10px]">Rest / Sleep</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-amber-950/60 rounded border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-300">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-sm">8 Hrs</div>\n                        <div class="text-[10px]">School + Study</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-emerald-950/60 rounded border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-300">\n                        <div class="font-bold text-sm">8 Hrs</div>\n                        <div class="text-[10px]">Play, Food & Life</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Personal Time Auditing</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">By plotting their own day on grid paper, students discover whether screen time or unstructured delays eat into vital sleep or physical activity.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/30 text-sky-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Mathematical Reflection:</strong> Fraction of day spent sleeping = 8/24 = 1/3 of the day.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the ratio of sleeping hours (8) to total daily educational hours (6 school + 2 study = 8)?', 'options': ['8 to 4 (2:1)', '8 to 8 (1:1 equal balance)', '8 to 6', '8 to 12'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Both sleep (8 hours) and combined education (8 hours) are equal, creating a 1:1 balance.', 'hint': 'Compare 8 hours of sleep with 8 hours of education.'}, {'question': 'If a student sleeps for 9 hours instead of 8, how many hours remain for waking activities?', 'options': ['14 hours', '15 hours', '16 hours', '12 hours'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Subtract sleep hours from the 24-hour total: 24 - 9 = 15 hours remaining.', 'hint': 'Subtract 9 from 24.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Finger Length Measurement', 'tagline': 'Hands-on Physical Data • Cutting and Measuring Paper Strips', 'lecture': "Data does not always arrive in printed survey sheets; frequently, students generate data through direct physical measurement. Consider a lively classroom inquiry where classmates investigate the question: whose index finger is longest in our group? To measure finger lengths accurately without rigid plastic rulers slipping, students employ flexible paper strips. Each child places a thin strip of paper along the inner length of their dominant index finger, starting from the base joint crease to the tip. The exact endpoint at the tip of the finger is marked cleanly with a sharp pencil. Using scissors, the student cuts or tears off the extra paper beyond the mark, creating a custom strip equal to their finger length. Next, each student places their strip against a standardized centimeter scale to record the length in centimeters and millimeters with precision. One classmate's strip measures four and a half centimeters, another measures five centimeters, while another reaches five point eight centimeters. Each paper strip is labeled clearly with the corresponding student's identification code for systematic record keeping. This tactile procedure bridges physical reality with mathematical abstraction. Collecting accurate measurements through standardized physical tools ensures data integrity before visual graphing begins across the class.", 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Paper Strip Measurement Protocol</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Flexible paper strips mold cleanly along finger contours, avoiding parallax errors caused by stiff rulers:</p>\n                <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 text-slate-300 mt-2 font-mono text-[11px]">\n                    <li>Align strip edge with the finger base crease joint</li>\n                    <li>Mark the exact fingertip apex with a fine pencil dot</li>\n                    <li>Cut / tear away the excess paper beyond the pencil mark</li>\n                    <li>Measure the strip on a millimeter ruler and record length</li>\n                </ol>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Sample Classroom Measurements</span>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-200 mt-1">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">Friend A: 4.5 cm</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">Friend B: 5.0 cm</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">Friend C: 5.8 cm</div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">Friend D: 4.2 cm</div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Accuracy Tip:</strong> Consistent starting points at the base joint are essential for valid comparisons.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why is a flexible paper strip advantageous when measuring body contours like fingers?', 'options': ['It cannot be cut with scissors', 'It conforms smoothly along curved surfaces without slipping', 'It always measures exactly 10 cm automatically', 'It makes the finger grow longer'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Flexible paper bends along curved anatomical surfaces, ensuring accurate end-to-end measurement.', 'hint': 'Consider how stiff rulers behave on curved skin.'}, {'question': "If Friend D's finger strip measures 4.2 cm and Friend C's measures 5.8 cm, what is the difference in length?", 'options': ['1.0 cm', '1.6 cm', '2.0 cm', '0.6 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Subtract Friend D's length from Friend C's: 5.8 - 4.2 = 1.6 cm.", 'hint': 'Calculate 5.8 minus 4.2.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Strip Bar Graph', 'tagline': 'Constructing Physical Graphs • Pasting Strips onto a Vertical Grid Axis', 'lecture': 'Once all student finger strips are trimmed and measured, the class constructs a physical bar graph directly on chart paper. Instead of drawing ink bars with a pen, the students physically paste their paper strips side by side along a horizontal baseline. A vertical reference line is marked with centimeter intervals from zero up to seven centimeters. Each child pastes their labeled strip standing vertically from the baseline, keeping the width and spacing completely uniform. Suddenly, the physical paper strips themselves become the vertical bars of an authentic, tangible bar graph. Looking across the chart, the varying heights of the paper strips immediately reveal the comparative distribution. The tallest strip rises to five point eight centimeters, belonging to the classmate with the longest index finger. The shortest strip reaches only four point two centimeters, belonging to the classmate with the shortest index finger. Subtracting four point two from five point eight reveals a spread of one point six centimeters across the measured cohort. This hands-on creation of a physical bar graph cements the foundational mathematical truth that bar graphs are visual representations of physical quantities. Physical graphs make abstract coordinate systems tangible and intuitive for every young learner.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Building a Tangible Physical Bar Graph</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Pasting physical strips of measured paper onto a common baseline transforms real objects into a live bar graph:</p>\n                <div class="mt-2 p-2 rounded bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 font-mono text-[11px] text-center">\n                    📏 Common Baseline: All strips start at Height = 0 cm\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Cohort Statistical Highlights</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Max Length (Tallest Strip):</span>\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">5.8 cm (Friend C)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Min Length (Shortest Strip):</span>\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">4.2 cm (Friend D)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="flex justify-between items-center p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>Variation Range (Spread):</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">5.8 - 4.2 = 1.6 cm</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Aligning all strips to a common zero line is mandatory for legitimate visual comparison.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What would happen if paper strips were pasted without aligning them to a common zero baseline?', 'options': ['The graph would become more accurate', 'Visual comparison would be invalid because base heights would differ', 'The strips would shrink automatically', 'The centimeter scale would turn negative'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'A common baseline is mandatory; otherwise, higher starting points create misleading optical heights.', 'hint': 'Think about whether a runner can start 5 meters ahead in a race.'}, {'question': 'What is the difference between the longest finger (5.8 cm) and shortest finger (4.2 cm)?', 'options': ['1.2 cm', '1.6 cm', '2.0 cm', '0.8 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '5.8 cm - 4.2 cm = 1.6 cm.', 'hint': 'Subtract 4.2 from 5.8.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Canteen Food Waste', 'tagline': 'Real-World Sustainability Data • Daily Waste Tracking in Kilograms', 'lecture': 'Data analysis becomes particularly meaningful and impactful when applied to pressing real-world issues, such as environmental sustainability and cafeteria food conservation. In this school study, kitchen staff monitors daily food waste collected from the student canteen across five consecutive weekdays. Every single afternoon, the kitchen staff weighs uneaten food scraped from plates and records the total mass in kilograms on a bulletin board. A vertical bar graph is displayed on the main school notice board to inform all students and teachers about weekly food waste patterns. The horizontal axis lists the weekdays and corresponding lunch menus: Monday sandwich, Tuesday paratha, Wednesday khichdi, Thursday puri-sabzi, and Friday idli-sambar. The vertical axis indicates the waste in kilograms, marked clearly from zero up to ten kilograms. On Monday, sandwich day recorded four kilograms of plate waste. On Tuesday, paratha day recorded seven kilograms of plate waste. On Wednesday, khichdi day saw waste decline sharply to three kilograms, the lowest of the entire school week. On Thursday, puri-sabzi day recorded eight kilograms, reaching the highest waste level of the entire week. On Friday, idli-sambar day resulted in five kilograms of waste. Observing these bar heights stimulates thoughtful inquiry into why certain meals result in substantially higher waste across the dining hall.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">School Canteen Waste Audit</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Daily kitchen waste recorded across weekdays to evaluate portion sizing and meal popularity:</p>\n                <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-2">\n                    <table class="w-full text-left font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300 border-collapse">\n                        <thead>\n                            <tr class="border-b border-slate-700 text-amber-300">\n                                <th class="py-1">Day</th>\n                                <th class="py-1">Menu Item</th>\n                                <th class="py-1 text-right">Waste (kg)</th>\n                            </tr>\n                        </thead>\n                        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800">\n                            <tr><td>Monday</td><td>Sandwich</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">4 kg</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Paratha</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">7 kg</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Khichdi</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-emerald-400">3 kg (Lowest)</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Puri-Sabzi</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-rose-400">8 kg (Highest)</td></tr>\n                            <tr><td>Friday</td><td>Idli-Sambar</td><td class="text-right font-bold text-white">5 kg</td></tr>\n                            <tr class="border-t border-slate-600 font-bold text-amber-400"><td>Total Week</td><td>All Menus</td><td class="text-right">27 kg</td></tr>\n                        </tbody>\n                    </table>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-rose-500/10 border border-rose-500/30 text-rose-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Peak Waste Alert:</strong> Thursday (Puri-Sabzi) generated 8 kg of plate waste, requiring immediate portion review.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which weekday generated the highest amount of food waste in the school canteen?', 'options': ['Monday (4 kg)', 'Tuesday (7 kg)', 'Thursday (8 kg)', 'Friday (5 kg)'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'Thursday (Puri-Sabzi) had 8 kg of waste, which is the tallest bar on the graph.', 'hint': 'Find the day with the highest recorded kilogram value.'}, {'question': 'How much less food was wasted on Wednesday (3 kg) compared to Thursday (8 kg)?', 'options': ['3 kg', '5 kg', '6 kg', '8 kg'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "Subtract Wednesday's waste from Thursday's waste: 8 - 3 = 5 kg less waste.", 'hint': 'Calculate 8 minus 3.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Waste Reduction Analysis', 'tagline': 'Quantifying Impact • Converting Waste Kilograms to Meals Saved', 'lecture': 'Let us take our canteen food waste analysis further by calculating the cumulative weekly impact and evaluating practical, sustainable solutions. To understand the total weekly toll, we sum the food waste across all five recorded school weekdays. Adding four kilograms on Monday, seven on Tuesday, three on Wednesday, eight on Thursday, and five on Friday yields twenty-seven kilograms in total. Twenty-seven kilograms of wholesome edible food discarded in a single school week represents a significant loss of precious agricultural resources. To grasp this waste in relatable human terms, we use an established nutritional benchmark: one kilogram of prepared food can feed three hungry children. Multiplying twenty-seven kilograms by three reveals that eighty-one children could have received a wholesome meal from the food discarded in just one week. Armed with this striking numerical data, the student council proposed three practical waste reduction strategies for the school. First, introduce flexible serving sizes, allowing students to request smaller initial portions and return for seconds if still hungry. Second, conduct student taste surveys to adjust recipes and seasoning for unpopular menu items. Third, display daily waste totals prominently in the dining hall to foster collective accountability. Mathematical data turns abstract numbers into compelling ethical catalysts for positive behavioral change.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Weekly Waste & Meal Conversion</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Converting mass of wasted food into equivalent student meals demonstrates the human dimension of statistics:</p>\n                <div class="space-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-2">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800 flex justify-between">\n                        <span>Total Weekly Waste:</span>\n                        <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold">4 + 7 + 3 + 8 + 5 = 27 kg</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800 flex justify-between">\n                        <span>Conversion Factor:</span>\n                        <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold">1 kg Food = 3 Child Meals</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800 flex justify-between">\n                        <span>Potential Meals Wasted:</span>\n                        <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold">27 × 3 = 81 Children Fed</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold block mb-1">Student Council Action Plan</span>\n                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 text-slate-300 text-[11px]">\n                    <li><b>Opt-in Sizing:</b> Serve smaller base portions with free seconds</li>\n                    <li><b>Menu Tuning:</b> Adjust seasoning based on student preference feedback</li>\n                    <li><b>Daily Notice:</b> Display daily kg tracker in canteen to raise mindfulness</li>\n                </ul>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Data for Action:</strong> Statistical analysis drives ethical, community-wide solutions.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 kg of food feeds 3 children, how many children could be fed with 27 kg of food?', 'options': ['54 children', '81 children', '90 children', '30 children'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'Multiply the total kilograms by the conversion rate: 27 × 3 = 81 children.', 'hint': 'Multiply 27 by 3.'}, {'question': 'What is the total food waste over the five school days?', 'options': ['20 kg', '25 kg', '27 kg', '32 kg'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': '4 + 7 + 3 + 8 + 5 = 27 kg total waste.', 'hint': 'Add 4, 7, 3, 8, and 5.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Picture Data Logic', 'tagline': 'Observational Counting • True or False Mathematical Verification', 'lecture': 'Data analysis also frequently involves verifying claims against detailed visual scenes, combining observational counting with rigorous logical reasoning. In this activity, students examine a lively illustration depicting a group of twelve school children gathered together in a sunny courtyard. The children wear various accessories and hold diverse school items: some wear caps, some wear spectacles, some carry backpacks, and some hold books. Students are presented with several statements to evaluate as True or False based strictly on careful visual counts. Consider the first statement: exactly five children are wearing caps. Scanning the illustration carefully, we count one, two, three, four, and five children sporting caps, confirming the statement as True. Consider a second claim: more children are wearing spectacles than are carrying backpacks. Counting spectacles reveals four children with glasses, while counting backpacks reveals six children with bags. Since four is strictly less than six, the claim is proven False. Consider a third statement: three children are wearing both a cap and spectacles simultaneously. Inspecting the intersection of features, we find exactly two children possessing both accessories, proving the claim False. Systematic observation trains young learners to scrutinize visual details methodically and resist hasty, unsubstantiated conclusions.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">Visual Observation Matrix (12 Children)</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">Methodical attribute counting across a cohort of 12 students in a courtyard illustration:</p>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">🧢 Wearing Caps: <b>5</b></div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">👓 Wearing Glasses: <b>4</b></div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">🎒 Wearing Backpacks: <b>6</b></div>\n                    <div class="p-1.5 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">📚 Holding Books: <b>7</b></div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Logical Statement Verification</span>\n                <div class="space-y-1 font-mono text-[10.5px] text-slate-300">\n                    <div class="p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>"5 children wear caps":</span> <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold ml-1">TRUE (Count = 5)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>"More glasses than backpacks":</span> <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold ml-1">FALSE (4 < 6)</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-1 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span>"Both cap and glasses = 3":</span> <span class="text-rose-400 font-bold ml-1">FALSE (Overlap = 2)</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/30 text-sky-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Scientific Rigor:</strong> Mathematical truth depends entirely on verifiable evidence, not superficial impressions.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 4 children wear glasses and 6 wear backpacks, which statement is mathematically True?', 'options': ['Glasses count exceeds backpack count', 'Backpack count exceeds glasses count by 2', 'Both counts are equal', 'There are 15 children wearing glasses'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': '6 backpacks - 4 glasses = 2 more children wearing backpacks than glasses.', 'hint': 'Compare 6 with 4.'}, {'question': 'What is the total number of children in the courtyard illustration?', 'options': ['10 children', '12 children', '15 children', '20 children'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': 'The cohort has exactly 12 children in the courtyard group.', 'hint': 'Check the total group size mentioned in the text.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Geometric Cutout Solids', 'tagline': 'Spatial Geometry Nets • Constructing Icosahedrons and Dodecahedrons', 'lecture': 'We conclude our textbook journey by examining the supplementary geometric cutout nets that bridge flat two-dimensional data with three-dimensional space. A geometric net is a two-dimensional flat pattern that can be folded along scored edges to construct a hollow three-dimensional solid polyhedron. In the textbook appendix, students explore the nets of two remarkable regular polyhedra: the icosahedron and the dodecahedron. The icosahedron net is composed entirely of twenty congruent equilateral triangles joined along shared edges. When carefully folded and joined with adhesive tabs, these twenty triangular faces form a symmetrical solid with twelve vertices and thirty edges. In contrast, the dodecahedron net consists of twelve congruent regular pentagons arranged in two interlocking flower-like clusters. Folding this net encloses a beautiful spherical solid featuring twelve pentagonal faces, twenty vertices, and thirty straight edges. These geometric solids exemplify regular polyhedra, where every single face is an identical regular polygon and every vertex joins equal numbers of faces. From counting daily TV hours to folding twenty-faced polyhedra, mathematics weaves a continuous tapestry connecting numbers, geometric shapes, and the physical world. Through pictures, tallies, graphs, and spatial nets, we learn to perceive structure, beauty, and truth throughout our universe.', 'content_html': '\n        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-amber-400 font-bold block mb-1">What is a Polyhedral Net?</span>\n                <p class="text-slate-300">A geometric net is an unfolded flat 2D pattern of connected polygons that can be folded along shared edges to form a closed 3D solid polyhedron.</p>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900/90 rounded-xl border border-slate-700">\n                <span class="text-cyan-400 font-bold block mb-1">Two Classic Regular Polyhedra</span>\n                <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-300 mt-1">\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-indigo-400 font-bold block">🔷 Icosahedron</span>\n                        <div>• 20 Equilateral Triangles</div>\n                        <div>• 12 Vertices, 30 Edges</div>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class="p-2 bg-slate-950/80 rounded border border-slate-800">\n                        <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold block">⬟ Dodecahedron</span>\n                        <div>• 12 Regular Pentagons</div>\n                        <div>• 20 Vertices, 30 Edges</div>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n            <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/10 border border-amber-500/30 text-amber-200 text-[11px]">\n                <strong>Euler\'s Formula:</strong> For both solids: Faces - Edges + Vertices = 2 (e.g., 20 - 30 + 12 = 2).\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        ', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many equilateral triangular faces make up a regular icosahedron?', 'options': ['12 faces', '20 faces', '30 faces', '8 faces'], 'answer': 1, 'explanation': "An icosahedron has exactly 20 congruent equilateral triangular faces (from the Greek 'eikosi' meaning twenty).", 'hint': "The prefix 'icosa' means twenty."}, {'question': 'What regular polygon shape forms all 12 faces of a dodecahedron?', 'options': ['Equilateral triangle', 'Square', 'Regular pentagon', 'Regular hexagon'], 'answer': 2, 'explanation': 'A dodecahedron has 12 faces that are all congruent regular 5-sided pentagons.', 'hint': 'Think of a 5-sided polygon.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 1 • Raw Data, Tally Bundles & TV Survey Frequency Tables', 'lecture': '', 'content_html': '', 'questions': [{'question': 'What is the primary defining feature of raw data?', 'options': ['It has already been plotted on a finished bar graph', 'It is initial unorganized numbers collected directly from observations', 'It is always a set of exactly 100 percentages', 'It only contains numbers that have been rounded'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think of notes scribbled in a notebook before any sorting.', 'explanation': 'Raw data represents original, unorganized information collected directly from source observations before categorization.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. By definition, raw data consists of original, unprocessed observations.<br>2. Before data is arranged in tables or graphs, it is in 'raw' form."}, {'question': 'In standard tally mark notation, what does a diagonal line across four vertical lines (<s>||||</s>) represent?', 'options': ['A group of 4 items', 'A group of 5 items', 'A group of 6 items', 'A group of 10 items'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Count the four strokes plus the fifth crossing slash.', 'explanation': 'Four vertical strokes crossed by one diagonal slash represents a bundle of exactly 5 tally marks.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Four vertical lines = 4 units.<br>2. One crossing diagonal line = 1 unit.<br>3. Total bundle = 4 + 1 = 5 units.'}, {'question': 'How many items are represented by three full tally bundles and two single marks (<s>||||</s> <s>||||</s> <s>||||</s> ||)?', 'options': ['15 items', '17 items', '18 items', '12 items'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Multiply 3 bundles by 5, then add 2.', 'explanation': '3 bundles × 5 = 15. Adding 2 single marks: 15 + 2 = 17 items.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 3 full bundles × 5 items/bundle = 15 items.<br>2. 2 individual strokes = 2 items.<br>3. Total count = 15 + 2 = 17 items.'}, {'question': 'In the 35-classmate TV viewing survey, how many children watched TV for 1 hour?', 'options': ['5 children', '8 children', '12 children', '6 children'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Look for the category with the highest frequency.', 'explanation': 'The frequency table records 12 children in the 1-hour viewing category.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. In the survey data: ½ hr = 5, 1 hr = 12, 1½ hrs = 8, 2 hrs = 6, >2 hrs = 4.<br>2. The 1-hour row has 12 children.'}, {'question': 'How many total children watch TV for 1 hour or less per day in the 35-student survey?', 'options': ['12 children', '17 children', '18 children', '25 children'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add the counts for ½ hour and 1 hour.', 'explanation': '5 (½ hr) + 12 (1 hr) = 17 children watch one hour or less.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Category ½ hour = 5 children.<br>2. Category 1 hour = 12 children.<br>3. Combined: 5 + 12 = 17 children.'}, {'question': 'How many total children watch TV for more than 1 hour per day?', 'options': ['17 children', '18 children', '20 children', '14 children'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Sum the categories: 1½ hrs, 2 hrs, and >2 hrs.', 'explanation': '8 (1½ hrs) + 6 (2 hrs) + 4 (>2 hrs) = 18 children watch more than one hour.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Category 1½ hrs = 8.<br>2. Category 2 hrs = 6.<br>3. Category >2 hrs = 4.<br>4. Total = 8 + 6 + 4 = 18 children.'}, {'question': 'What is the check sum of the frequencies for all 5 time intervals (5 + 12 + 8 + 6 + 4)?', 'options': ['30', '35', '40', '50'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Add all 5 frequency numbers together.', 'explanation': '5 + 12 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 35, matching the total number of surveyed classmates.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 5 + 12 = 17.<br>2. 17 + 8 = 25.<br>3. 25 + 6 = 31.<br>4. 31 + 4 = 35 total respondents.'}, {'question': 'What percentage of the surveyed cohort of 35 children watch TV for more than 2 hours if 4 children are in that group?', 'options': ['4/35 of the cohort', '12/35 of the cohort', '1/2 of the cohort', '35/4 of the cohort'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Write the count over the total surveyed population.', 'explanation': 'Exactly 4 out of the 35 surveyed children fall in the more than 2 hours category, represented as 4/35.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Count in category = 4.<br>2. Total population = 35.<br>3. Fraction = 4/35.'}, {'question': 'Why must categories in a frequency table be mutually exclusive?', 'options': ['So that each student response is counted in exactly one category without overlap', 'So that the numbers are always multiples of 10', 'To ensure every category has the same number of students', 'To eliminate the need for tally marks'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Consider what happens if someone fits into two categories at once.', 'explanation': 'Mutually exclusive categories ensure each observation belongs to one and only one group, preventing double counting.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 'Mutually exclusive' means categories do not overlap.<br>2. If categories overlapped, a single child could be counted twice, corrupting the survey sum."}, {'question': 'If 18 out of 35 children watch more than 1 hour of TV daily, is it True that more than half the children exceed 1 hour?', 'options': ['True (since half of 35 is 17.5, and 18 > 17.5)', 'False (since 18 is less than half)', 'Cannot be determined without knowing their ages', 'False because 35 is an odd number'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Calculate half of 35.', 'explanation': 'Half of 35 is 17.5. Since 18 is greater than 17.5, more than half the children watch more than 1 hour daily.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Half of 35 = 35 ÷ 2 = 17.5.<br>2. Actual count = 18.<br>3. 18 > 17.5, so the statement is True.'}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 2 • Pictograph Keys, Re-scaling & Traffic Observation Tallies', 'lecture': '', 'content_html': '', 'questions': [{'question': 'What is the primary function of a Key in a pictograph?', 'options': ['To lock the document so no one can edit it', 'To state how many physical units each picture symbol represents', 'To list the names of the students who drew the pictures', 'To specify the color of paint to use'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Look for the scaling explanation.', 'explanation': 'The Key or Scale defines the numerical value that each individual icon symbol represents.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. In pictographs, symbols represent scaled quantities.<br>2. The Key provides the translation factor from symbols to real-world units.'}, {'question': 'In a toy shop pictograph where 1 star = 5 items, how many board games are represented by 6 star icons?', 'options': ['11 items', '25 items', '30 items', '60 items'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Multiply 6 by 5.', 'explanation': '6 icons × 5 items per icon = 30 board games.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Number of icons = 6.<br>2. Scale per icon = 5.<br>3. Total = 6 × 5 = 30 board games.'}, {'question': 'How many icons must be drawn to represent 40 sports gear items when the scale is 1 icon = 5 items?', 'options': ['4 icons', '8 icons', '10 icons', '20 icons'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide 40 by 5.', 'explanation': '40 items ÷ 5 items per icon = 8 icons.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Total items = 40.<br>2. Scale = 5.<br>3. Icons needed = 40 ÷ 5 = 8 icons.'}, {'question': 'If the toy shop has 90 total items and changes its scale from 1 icon = 5 items to 1 icon = 10 items, how many icons are needed under the new scale?', 'options': ['18 icons', '9 icons', '45 icons', '10 icons'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide 90 by 10.', 'explanation': '90 items ÷ 10 items per icon = 9 icons.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Total items = 90.<br>2. New scale = 10.<br>3. Number of icons = 90 ÷ 10 = 9 icons.'}, {'question': 'In the street traffic survey, 1 scooter symbol represents 3 two-wheelers. How many vehicles passed on Monday if 5 symbols were drawn?', 'options': ['8 vehicles', '15 vehicles', '18 vehicles', '21 vehicles'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Multiply 5 by 3.', 'explanation': '5 symbols × 3 vehicles per symbol = 15 two-wheelers.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Number of symbols = 5.<br>2. Scale = 3.<br>3. Total count = 5 × 3 = 15 vehicles.'}, {'question': 'On Friday, 8 scooter symbols were recorded (scale: 1 symbol = 3 vehicles). How many vehicles passed on Friday?', 'options': ['11 vehicles', '16 vehicles', '24 vehicles', '27 vehicles'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Multiply 8 by 3.', 'explanation': '8 symbols × 3 vehicles per symbol = 24 two-wheelers.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Number of symbols = 8.<br>2. Scale = 3.<br>3. Total count = 8 × 3 = 24 vehicles.'}, {'question': 'How many more two-wheelers passed on Friday (24) than on Wednesday (9)?', 'options': ['12 vehicles', '15 vehicles', '18 vehicles', '21 vehicles'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Subtract 9 from 24.', 'explanation': '24 - 9 = 15 more vehicles passed on Friday than on Wednesday.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Friday count = 24.<br>2. Wednesday count = 9.<br>3. Difference = 24 - 9 = 15 vehicles.'}, {'question': 'What is the total number of two-wheelers observed across Monday (15), Wednesday (9), and Friday (24)?', 'options': ['42 vehicles', '45 vehicles', '48 vehicles', '54 vehicles'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Add 15, 9, and 24.', 'explanation': '15 + 9 + 24 = 48 two-wheelers in total.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 15 + 9 = 24.<br>2. 24 + 24 = 48 vehicles.'}, {'question': 'If 1 icon represents 6 vehicles, how many icons represent 24 vehicles?', 'options': ['2 icons', '3 icons', '4 icons', '6 icons'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Divide 24 by 6.', 'explanation': '24 ÷ 6 = 4 full icons.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 24 vehicles ÷ 6 vehicles/icon = 4 icons.'}, {'question': "Under a scale of 1 icon = 6 vehicles, how is Monday's count of 15 vehicles represented?", 'options': ['2 full icons only', '2 full icons plus 1 half-icon (6 + 6 + 3 = 15)', '3 full icons', '1 full icon plus 1 quarter-icon'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': '15 = 12 + 3. Notice that 3 is half of 6.', 'explanation': '15 ÷ 6 = 2 with a remainder of 3. Since 3 is half of 6, it is drawn as 2½ icons.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 2 full icons = 2 × 6 = 12.<br>2. 1 half-icon = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.<br>3. Total = 12 + 3 = 15 vehicles.'}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 3 • 24-Hour Routines, Bar Graphs & Finger Strip Measurements', 'lecture': '', 'content_html': '', 'questions': [{'question': 'In a 24-hour daily routine, if a student sleeps 8 hours, what fraction of the full day is spent sleeping?', 'options': ['1/4 of the day', '1/3 of the day', '1/2 of the day', '2/3 of the day'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Divide 8 by 24 and simplify the fraction.', 'explanation': '8 ÷ 24 = 8/24 = 1/3 of the day.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Sleeping hours = 8.<br>2. Total hours in a day = 24.<br>3. Fraction = 8/24 = 1/3.'}, {'question': 'In a vertical bar graph, which axis displays the numerical scale (such as hours or centimeters)?', 'options': ['The horizontal baseline', 'The vertical axis (Y-axis)', 'The chart title', 'The legend box'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think about which direction the bars rise.', 'explanation': 'In a standard vertical bar graph, the vertical axis (Y-axis) carries the numerical scale, determining the height of each bar.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Horizontal axis holds categorical labels.<br>2. Vertical axis holds the quantitative numerical scale.'}, {'question': 'In the daily routine graph, School is 6 hours and Self-Study is 2 hours. How many total hours are dedicated to learning?', 'options': ['4 hours', '6 hours', '8 hours', '10 hours'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Add 6 and 2.', 'explanation': '6 + 2 = 8 hours total for educational activities.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. School hours = 6.<br>2. Self-study hours = 2.<br>3. Total learning hours = 6 + 2 = 8 hours.'}, {'question': 'What is the check sum of all 5 daily routine activities: 8 (sleep) + 6 (school) + 2 (study) + 4 (play/food) + 4 (other)?', 'options': ['20 hours', '22 hours', '24 hours', '26 hours'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'A full day has 24 hours.', 'explanation': '8 + 6 + 2 + 4 + 4 = 24 hours, accounting for the entire day.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 8 + 6 = 14.<br>2. 14 + 2 = 16.<br>3. 16 + 4 = 20.<br>4. 20 + 4 = 24 hours.'}, {'question': 'What is the primary visual advantage of a bar graph over an icon list?', 'options': ['Bar graphs require using multiple paint colors', 'Bar heights allow immediate visual comparison of quantities without counting icons', 'Bar graphs can only be drawn on circular paper', 'Bar graphs do not need any numerical labels'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Consider how quickly your eyes can compare two heights.', 'explanation': 'Bar graphs allow instantaneous visual comparison of quantities via bar heights without manual counting.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. With icons, you must count each symbol to find the value.<br>2. With bars, the relative height immediately communicates which category is larger.'}, {'question': 'Why did students use flexible paper strips instead of stiff plastic rulers to measure their index fingers?', 'options': ['Paper strips bend smoothly along curved skin contours from base crease to tip', 'Plastic rulers are not allowed in mathematics class', 'Paper strips make fingers grow longer during measurement', 'Stiff rulers always break when measuring skin'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about measuring along curved anatomical shapes.', 'explanation': 'Flexible paper strips conform smoothly along curved body contours, avoiding parallax and slippage errors.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Fingers have natural curves and creases.<br>2. Flexible strips mold along the surface for an accurate end-to-end measure.'}, {'question': "In the finger strip activity, Friend C's strip was 5.8 cm and Friend D's was 4.2 cm. What was the length difference?", 'options': ['1.2 cm', '1.4 cm', '1.6 cm', '1.8 cm'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Subtract 4.2 from 5.8.', 'explanation': '5.8 cm - 4.2 cm = 1.6 cm difference.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Longest strip = 5.8 cm.<br>2. Shortest strip = 4.2 cm.<br>3. Difference = 5.8 - 4.2 = 1.6 cm.'}, {'question': 'Why must all paper strips be pasted starting from the exact same horizontal baseline?', 'options': ['To ensure that visual strip heights accurately reflect true finger lengths without offset bias', 'To prevent the paper strips from falling off the chart', 'So that all strips end up having the same length', 'To save glue during the activity'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'What happens if one runner starts 2 meters ahead in a race?', 'explanation': 'A common zero baseline ensures that the visible height of every strip is directly proportional to its true length.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. If strips started at different heights, a shorter strip placed higher would look taller.<br>2. A common baseline (height = 0) is required for valid comparative graphing.'}, {'question': "If Friend A has a finger length of 4.5 cm and Friend B has 5.0 cm, how much longer is Friend B's finger?", 'options': ['0.3 cm', '0.5 cm', '1.0 cm', '0.2 cm'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Subtract 4.5 from 5.0.', 'explanation': '5.0 cm - 4.5 cm = 0.5 cm (or 5 millimeters).', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 5.0 - 4.5 = 0.5 cm.'}, {'question': 'How many millimeters are in 5.8 centimeters?', 'options': ['58 mm', '580 mm', '5.8 mm', '0.58 mm'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Multiply centimeters by 10.', 'explanation': '1 cm = 10 mm, so 5.8 cm × 10 = 58 mm.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 1 cm = 10 mm.<br>2. 5.8 × 10 = 58 mm.'}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Challenge 4 • Canteen Waste Auditing, Observational Logic & 3D Nets', 'lecture': '', 'content_html': '', 'questions': [{'question': 'In the canteen food waste study, which menu day produced the highest waste (8 kg)?', 'options': ['Monday (Sandwich)', 'Tuesday (Paratha)', 'Wednesday (Khichdi)', 'Thursday (Puri-Sabzi)'], 'answer': 3, 'hint': 'Look for the day with 8 kg on the notice board.', 'explanation': 'Thursday (Puri-Sabzi) produced 8 kg of waste, the highest of the week.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. The recorded values are: Mon = 4 kg, Tue = 7 kg, Wed = 3 kg, Thu = 8 kg, Fri = 5 kg.<br>2. The maximum is 8 kg on Thursday.'}, {'question': 'Which menu day produced the lowest food waste (3 kg)?', 'options': ['Monday (Sandwich)', 'Wednesday (Khichdi)', 'Thursday (Puri-Sabzi)', 'Friday (Idli-Sambar)'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Look for the day with 3 kg.', 'explanation': 'Wednesday (Khichdi) produced only 3 kg of food waste, the lowest of the week.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 3 kg on Wednesday is the minimum value recorded across all five days.'}, {'question': 'What was the total food waste across all five school days (4 + 7 + 3 + 8 + 5)?', 'options': ['22 kg', '25 kg', '27 kg', '30 kg'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Add the five daily waste numbers.', 'explanation': '4 + 7 + 3 + 8 + 5 = 27 kg total weekly waste.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 4 + 7 = 11.<br>2. 11 + 3 = 14.<br>3. 14 + 8 = 22.<br>4. 22 + 5 = 27 kg.'}, {'question': 'If 1 kg of prepared food can feed 3 hungry children, how many children could be fed with the 27 kg of wasted food?', 'options': ['54 children', '72 children', '81 children', '90 children'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': 'Multiply 27 by 3.', 'explanation': '27 kg × 3 children per kg = 81 children could have been fed.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Total waste = 27 kg.<br>2. Feeding rate = 3 children/kg.<br>3. 27 × 3 = 81 children.'}, {'question': 'How much less food was wasted on Wednesday (3 kg) than on Tuesday (7 kg)?', 'options': ['3 kg', '4 kg', '5 kg', '2 kg'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Subtract 3 from 7.', 'explanation': '7 kg - 3 kg = 4 kg less waste on Wednesday.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Tuesday = 7 kg.<br>2. Wednesday = 3 kg.<br>3. Difference = 7 - 3 = 4 kg.'}, {'question': 'In the courtyard picture logic puzzle, 4 children wear glasses and 6 wear backpacks. Which statement is False?', 'options': ['Backpack wearers exceed glasses wearers by 2', 'There are more children wearing glasses than backpacks', 'There are 6 children wearing backpacks', 'There are 4 children wearing glasses'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Compare 4 with 6.', 'explanation': '4 is less than 6, so claiming glasses wearers exceed backpack wearers is False.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Glasses = 4.<br>2. Backpacks = 6.<br>3. 4 < 6, so 'more glasses than backpacks' is False."}, {'question': 'If 5 children wear caps in a group of 12 children, how many children do NOT wear caps?', 'options': ['5 children', '7 children', '8 children', '6 children'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Subtract 5 from 12.', 'explanation': '12 - 5 = 7 children do not wear caps.', 'solution_html': '<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. Total cohort = 12.<br>2. Wearing caps = 5.<br>3. Not wearing caps = 12 - 5 = 7 children.'}, {'question': 'What is a geometric net?', 'options': ['A fishing net used by mathematicians', 'A flat 2D pattern that can be folded to construct a 3D solid polyhedron', 'A computer network cable', 'A type of graph paper with circular dots only'], 'answer': 1, 'hint': 'Think of cutting paper and folding it into a box.', 'explanation': 'A geometric net is a flat 2D pattern of connected faces that folds along edges to enclose a 3D solid.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. In geometry, an unfolded flat layout of a solid's faces is called its net.<br>2. Folding along scored creases creates the 3D solid."}, {'question': 'How many equilateral triangular faces form the net of a regular icosahedron?', 'options': ['12 faces', '18 faces', '20 faces', '30 faces'], 'answer': 2, 'hint': "The Greek prefix 'icosa' means twenty.", 'explanation': 'An icosahedron has exactly 20 congruent equilateral triangular faces.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 'Icosa' = 20.<br>2. A regular icosahedron has 20 triangular faces, 12 vertices, and 30 edges."}, {'question': 'What polygon shape forms the 12 faces of a regular dodecahedron net?', 'options': ['Regular pentagons (5-sided)', 'Equilateral triangles', 'Squares', 'Regular hexagons'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of 5-sided shapes.', 'explanation': 'A dodecahedron is composed of 12 regular pentagonal faces.', 'solution_html': "<strong>Step-by-Step Derivation:</strong><br>1. 'Dodeca' = 12.<br>2. Each of the 12 faces is a regular 5-sided polygon (pentagon)."}]}]
